{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Twenty.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.795,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Scent of Lavender.",
   "start": 2.395,
   "end": 3.803,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes Bell has the best nose in Tidewhistle Cove. She can tell a breakfast tea from an afternoon blend from across the room, and she can tell when scones are one minute from burning.",
   "start": 5.203,
   "end": 16.211,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Monday, Tamsin Trevelyan asked her to come to the bookshop at once. Tamsin's grandfather's silver fountain pen had been taken from the little velvet lined drawer under the counter. Tamsin had shown it to three customers that morning, one after another, and each had held the drawer open and looked inside. When the last one had left, the pen was gone.",
   "start": 17.061,
   "end": 38.757,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The three customers had been Captain Quill, Hedley Truscott and Mrs Vosper, a visitor from up the coast.",
   "start": 39.607,
   "end": 46.029,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes bent over the open drawer and breathed in. The velvet smelled, quite strongly, of lavender water.",
   "start": 46.879,
   "end": 53.556,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Then she went to find the three customers.",
   "start": 54.406,
   "end": 56.561,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Captain Quill was on the quay helping to unload the morning's catch, and he smelled, as he nearly always did, of fish and seaweed.",
   "start": 57.411,
   "end": 65.453,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Hedley Truscott was in the boatyard with his head inside an outboard motor, and he smelled of engine oil and nothing else whatsoever.",
   "start": 66.303,
   "end": 74.325,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mrs Vosper was taking tea in the Kettle and Gull. As Agnes stepped through the door, a wave of lavender drifted across the room. Mrs Vosper wore a little sprig of lavender pinned to her collar, and she dabbed lavender water on her wrists as she spoke.",
   "start": 75.175,
   "end": 90.833,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"A pen?\" said Mrs Vosper. \"Oh, I barely glanced into that drawer. I certainly never touched anything inside it.\"",
   "start": 91.683,
   "end": 98.851,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who took the silver fountain pen?",
   "start": 99.951,
   "end": 102.81,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 104.01,
   "end": 105.034,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 105.934,
   "end": 111.161,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 115.661,
   "end": 116.685,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mrs Vosper took the pen.",
   "start": 117.785,
   "end": 119.491,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The velvet inside the drawer smelled strongly of lavender water. Of the three people who had looked into that drawer, only Mrs Vosper wears lavender. Captain Quill smelled of fish and seaweed, and Hedley of engine oil. If she had barely glanced in, her scent would not have soaked into the velvet. Someone wearing lavender water had reached right inside.",
   "start": 120.341,
   "end": 141.931,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mrs Vosper sighed and took the pen out of her handbag. She said she wrote all her letters with a fountain pen, and had never seen one so fine. Tamsin, who knew a fellow letter writer when she met one, sold her a good, plain fountain pen from the shop, and threw in a bottle of blue ink. The drawer smelled of lavender for a month.",
   "start": 142.781,
   "end": 163.346,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
